an old AS2100 (Sable) with four 275MHz CPU's and 1GB of RAM.  It was a 
beast back in the day, that's for sure.  The problem is that SMP on 
Sable hasn't worked in a really long time, since about kernel 2.2 days.  
And SMP is the only thing that makes that box attractive.  I have a 
couple of Alpha motherboards, one being an SX and one an LX, I think.  
Both are, I think, 533MHz CPUs.  I still have some of the Matrox video 
cards that work with them, as well as a dual SCSI/ethernet combo card 
that is bootable with the AlphaPC boards as well as the AS2100.  I still 
have a PCI DAC960 card in the AS2100, and even have the previous EISA 
DAC960 RAID controller, as the AS2100 has two RAID-connected 
Storageworks backplanes.

I did a Stage 1 Gentoo on the AS2100 about ten years ago (took a month), 
but ended up with a Debian with a 2.2 kernel on it, so that SMP would work.

Performance for the day was quite good, but that day is long past, 
unfortunately.



